Chapter: DiskFactsRecover
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The superblock is written twice, alternating between sector 0 and
 sector 1 on the parity of its generation number, and boot picks
 whichever copy is valid and newest. That layout exists for one reason:
 a machine that loses power partway through a superblock write must
 still find an intact older copy and come up on it. Nothing tested that
 the fallback works, and a double-buffered superblock whose second copy
 is never actually read is one copy with extra steps.

 The old body wrote nothing to disk. It built a FactStore in memory,
 stored five facts, loaded three back and printed the count -- a
 chapter named for disk recovery that never touched a sector.

 The corrupt-entry and multi-sector cases in the log itself are already
 pinned by disk-facts-multi-load; this is the other half, the
 superblock.

Section: Damage

 A superblock is recognised by its magic at offset 0. Overwriting one
 byte of it is what a torn write looks like from the reader's side: the
 sector is present and readable, and it is not a superblock.

  wreck-superblock : Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  wreck-superblock (sector) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector sector
    let old = peek-byte buf 0
    in let w = poke-byte buf 0 (if old == 88 then 89 else 88)
    in block-write-sector sector buf
  end

Section: Reporting

  found : Maybe Fact, Text -> Text
  found (m) (want) =
   when m
    is Just (f) -> if f.content == want then "ok" else "CORRUPTED"
    is None -> "lost"

  survey : DiskFactStore -> Text
  survey (store) =
   let a = found (disk-read-fact store (fact-content-hash "alpha")) "alpha"
   in let b = found (disk-read-fact store (fact-content-hash "bravo")) "bravo"
   in let c = found (disk-read-fact store (fact-content-hash "charlie")) "charlie"
   in let d = found (disk-read-fact store (fact-content-hash "delta")) "delta"
   in a & " " & b & " " & c & " " & d

Section: Entry

 Two checkpoints, so both superblock slots hold a real generation and
 they disagree about how far the log runs. Then the newer slot is torn.

 The fallback answer is deliberately NOT "all four facts". Charlie and
 delta are on the disk, intact and hash-verifiable, and the recovered
 store must not report them: the only record that the log extended that
 far went down with the torn superblock, and a reader that walked past
 its own log-head would be trusting sectors nothing vouches for. Losing
 the tail of an uncheckpointed log is the correct outcome. Reading it
 back anyway would look like a better result and would mean the
 log-head is not load-bearing.

  opening : [Device.Block, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let s0 = disk-init
    in let s1 = disk-write-fact s0 DefinitionFact "alpha" 1
    in let s2 = disk-write-fact s1 DefinitionFact "bravo" 2
    in let cp1 = disk-checkpoint s2
    in let s3 = disk-write-fact cp1 DefinitionFact "charlie" 3
    in let s4 = disk-write-fact s3 DefinitionFact "delta" 4
    in let cp2 = disk-checkpoint s4
    in act
      print-line-uni ("written=" & show (disk-fact-count cp2))
      let fresh = disk-load
      in act
        print-line-uni ("boot-count=" & show (disk-fact-count fresh))
        print-line-uni ("boot-facts=" & survey fresh)
      end
      torn <- wreck-superblock 1
      let fallback = disk-load
      in act
        print-line-uni ("fallback-count=" & show (disk-fact-count fallback))
        print-line-uni ("fallback-facts=" & survey fallback)
      end
      gone <- wreck-superblock 0
      let bare = disk-load
      in act
        print-line-uni ("bare-count=" & show (disk-fact-count bare))
        print-line-uni ("bare-facts=" & survey bare)
      end
    end
  end
